===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*þurnuz||thorn, sloe}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*trnus}}, {{m|ine-pro|*tr̥nom}} < {{m|ine-pro|*(s)tern-||thorny bush, thorn}}, a variant of the base {{m|ine-pro|*(s)ter-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*(s)terə-||rigid, stiff}}. Germanic cognates include {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|thorn}} ({{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|Dorn}}, {{m|nds|Doorn}}), {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|doorn}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|thorn}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Dorn}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|þorn}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|törne}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌽𐌿𐍃}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{etyl|cu|-}} {{m|cu|трънъ|sc=Cyrs}} ({{etyl|ru|-}} {{m|ru|тёрн||sloe, blackthorn}}, {{etyl|sk|-}} {{m|sk|tŕň}}), {{etyl|sa|-}} {{term|तृण||grass|sc=Deva}}.

